OVERVIEW

PTOMAC stands for "Pascal to C Macros". When interfacing Pascal generated code
to C, each function goes to the operating system or other library via a 
"wrapper" function that serves three basic purposes:

1. The calling convention for IP Pascal on the given CPU is converted to the
specific calling convention for the C based operating system or library to
be interfaced.

2. Differences in the format of parameters are converted to and from C format.
For example, C style zero terminated strings are converted to and from C to
Pascal address/length format.

3. Data structures are converted to and from the particular operating system
or library in use. For example, a structure containing a zero terminated
string is converted to Pascal format, etc.

The wrapper file, which is usually named for the particular operating system
or library in use, is a collection of such wrapper functions, along with
the startup code for that particular system, plus any equates, data or any
other objects needed.

MACRO FORMAT

The wrapper file started as an ad-hoc interface file. The next step in the
evolution of wrappers was the PTOCMAC package. PTOCMAC allows a typical call
from Pascal to C to be described in a fairly machine and calling convention
way:

ptocf sname, aname, return, param1, param1, ... paramn

The name of the macro stands for "Pascal to C function". This is somewhat of a
misnomer, because procedures are also described by specifing a void return value
for the function.

The meaning of each field in the macro is as follows:

sname

Contains the Pascal source name for the call. This is the name of the function
or procedure that the Pascal source will call. It is usually coined to make
the fully qualified name of the function or procedure. Typical coins are:

sc_name
windows_name
linux386_name

The module coin "sc_" is the old style, meaning "System Call". With the new
modular naming system, the coin is the same as the name of the containing
module.

aname

Contains the assembly name for the call. This is the name of the actual entry
point in the system or library that the wrapper function will call. It is not
automatically coined, but typically has the name of the module that will be
called. For example:

user32_drawtexta

Is the windows name for the getclientrect call, in the user32 module. The
trailing "a" means ASCII (vs. Unicode) in the windows call naming convention.

return

Gives the descriptor for the return value (which could be void).

ParamXX

Each parameter has a descriptor, which will be described below.

The macro format describes the function and its parameters in the same order as
the parameters appear. For a typical call (Windows):

function sc_getstdhandle(hdlrc: integer): integer; external;

The equivalent macro is:

ptocf getstdhandle, kernel32_getstdhandle, int, int

Going through the macro parameters in order, "getstdhandle" is coined to
"sc_getstdhandle" for the name of the function, "kernel32_getstandardhandle"
gives the Windows entry point name for the function, "int" gives the descriptor
of the return value (integer), and the final "int" gives the descriptor of the
parameter for the function (integer).

CLIENTS AND GENERATORS OF THE PTOCMAC FORMAT

The appearance of the macro generates a wrapper for the call. In this way, the
wrapper file can mix automatically generated function wrappers, manually
written function wrappers, other routines, and needed data to complete the 
entire wrapper file.

The calling convention and machine dependent conventions are contained within
the macro itself. In this way, ptocmac generation statements can be constructed
according to the format of the C call, and this same ptocmac statement used for
different machines and operating systems. Typically, if the operating system
involved is machine transportable, then so will the ptocmac statements be.
The ptocmac statements must be custom written for the particular operating
system or library in use, but the return and parameter descriptors can be 
reused, since they mostly refer to the C types being converted to and from.

Because of this, the parameter descriptors form a catalog that is standardized
even between different systems. For example, Windows and Linux use the same
descriptor names, even though the ptocmac files for each system are very
different, and generate for different calling conventions.

As said, the ptocmac package started as an augumentation for a manually created
wrapper file. The next stage of evolution was to automatically generate the 
macro descriptions automatically from C header files, which is performed by the
"ch2ph" program.

ptocmac relies on a very complex macro to create its wrapper functions. While
only one such macro exists, ptocf, it was planned that other macros are
possible, such as a macro that creates the callback functions that are currently
created manually. The ability to mix manual and automatic methods like this is
what makes the ptocmac system highly flexible.

When the ptocmac system originated, the ptomac system was much simpler, and it
was believed that it would be replaced with a Pascal program that would read
function descriptors and generate the wrappers using optimized placement of
parameters and registers. However, simple improvements to the macro capability
of AS have allowed ptocmac to grow to cover the increasing complexity required
from the calling convention, and ptocmac has a sufficient number of different
cases that generates reasonably efficient wrappers. This has the added benifit
that the macro generator in AS is improved and tested at the same time. When and
if the system is replaced by a Pascal program, it will likely use the same
format as the current macros, since that is standard between existing tools.

RETURN VALUE DESCRIPTORS

The return value descriptors tell if a return value is returned at all, and if
so, how to treat it. A function without a return value is the same as a 
procedure, so a Pascal procedure is essentially a function returning nothing,
just as C does.

void

Void indcates that there is no return value. It is a procedure.

int

The function returns a standard integer value, which usually fits in a single
word or register.

bool

The return value is converted from a C style boolean to Pascal style boolean.
If the value is 0, it is left alone, otherwise it is replaced by a 1.

ptr

The return value is a pointer. Usually treated identically to int.

err

Used in Unix, the return value is checked for negative. If it is negative, the
absolute value of it is placed in the global variable "errno", and is replaced
by -1 in the return. If it isn't negative, errno is cleared to 0, and the value
returned as is.

PARAMETER VALUE DESCRIPTORS

Parameter values are special, because they may have convertions performed both
before and after the call. The convertions performed before the call are "in"
convertions. The convertions performed after the call are "out" convertions.

int

The value passed is an integer, and has the same size as a word or register in
the current CPU model.

ptr

The value is an address of another data element. Occupies a word or register,
is probally identical to int. A separate code for pointers exists so that the
fact that the value represents a pointer is documented.

ptrl

The value is a tagged pointer, and is converted to an address followed by a
length for the C call.

lptr

The value is a tagged pointer, and is converted to a length followed by an
address for the C call.

ptrd

The value is a tagged pointer, but only the address is used for the C call. The
length is dropped.

str

The in value is a Pascal address/length string. The in convertion moves the
string to a buffer with a zero termination, and passes that to C.
This descriptor is not used in the current windows.asm file, with pstrz being
preferred. That removes any right padding first. str is typically needed only
where a very literal string needs to be passed, including any trailing spaces.

strl

The in value is a Pascal address/length string. The address with length
following is passed to C. This is the same as ptrl, but documents the fact that
characters are pointed to. No convertion or buffering is done.

lstr

The in value is a Pascal address/length string. The length, followed by the
address, is passed to C. This is the same as lptr, but documents the fact that
characters are pointed to. No convertion or buffering is done.

pstrz

The in value is a Pascal address/length string. A buffer is created, and the 
string placed there, without the right hand space padding, then the string is
zero terminated, and passed to C as the string address only.

zstrp

A Pascal address/length string is passed for use as an out parameter. On return,
the zero terminated string in the buffer has the zero removed, then the string
is padded right with spaces to fit the full string length. The address and
length is passed to C.

lzstrp

A Pascal address/length string is passed for use as an out parameter. On return,
the zero terminated string in the buffer has the zero removed, then the string
is padded right with spaces to fit the full string length. As zstrp, but the
length, followed by the address is passed to C.

zstrpd

As zstrp, but the length is dropped on the C call. This descriptor is rarely
used, with zstrp being preferred.

lstro

The in value is a Pascal address/length string. The address with length
following is passed to C. If the mode is unencoded, this directive is identical
to strl. In encoded mode, the string is translated to encoded on output.

strlz

The same as zstrp, for Linux. This needs to be merged.

zer

This code does not mark an input parameter at all, but places a zero in the
corresponding C calling order. This is used to mark an unused parameter in the
C function. Typically it is used with overloads.

one

This code does not mark an input parameter at all, but places a one in the
corresponding C calling order. This is used to mark an unused parameter in the
C function. Typically it is used with overloads.

bool

The value passed is a Pascal boolean value. Because Pascal values are already
limited to 0 and 1, this is effectively the same as int and ptr. Used to
document the type of a parameter.

wclsptr

The value passed is a Windows class record pointer. The embedded Pascal string
field(s) in the record are converted to zero terminated strings by copying the
entire record and replacing the strings. Used in Windows only.

evl

The value passed is a Pascal string set for environment, which is a series of
linked string records to set the environment in Windows. Windows expects a 
buffer filled with zero terminated string data, so the string set is converted
into a buffer and send via the C function. Windows only.
